\subsection{The knowledge part}

The questions of the knowledge test are written against the course's
intended learning outcomes (\cref{prior-knowledge}), and published
here in full.
They are inspired by the earlier course instances' diagnostic tests,
written by Emma Riese and Camilla Björn.
There is one question per outcome area: control structures, containers
and references, usable interaction, dividing programs into functions,
files, error handling, classes, and reviewing others' code.
Each question tests one concept, with the distractors expressing common
misconceptions about it, and all weigh one point: we measure knowledge
by counting correct answers.

The items are numbered from position 11, so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps
are fine, and the numbering leaves room for quiz-specific items before
them, such as the end quiz's preparation item.

<<knowledge-part items>>=
<<item: tracing a loop>>,
<<item: two names one list>>,
<<item: asking for input>>,
<<item: repeated code>>,
<<item: reading a file>>,
<<item: a crashing answer>>,
<<item: classes and objects>>,
<<item: reviewing a program>>
