\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:quiz-analysis}

Canvas compiles the responses into a student-analysis report: a CSV
file with one row per student and one column per item, keyed by the
item titles above.
The analysis program fetches that report itself---we know the course
and the quiz titles, so nothing needs to be clicked or downloaded by
hand---using the same [[canvaslms]] machinery as the command-line tool.
It then tabulates the closed items and writes the open-text answers to
a coding sheet: a CSV file with one row per student and columns for the
codes.
We code all the data we asked for, so that every collected answer is
usable: from the languages answer the languages themselves, roughly how
much they were used, and whether Python is among them; from the
debugging exercise whether the described steps exhibit contrast,
generalisation or fusion thinking (\cref{analysis,mq:agreement}).

The coding is done by a human, but a large language model prepares it:
with [[--llm]] the program asks a model (via the [[llm]] package) to
suggest the codes for each answer, and the human coder verifies or
overrides them in the sheet.
The suggested codes are kept in separate columns from the verified
ones, so the sheet always shows which is which.

<<[[analyze_quiz.py]]>>=
"""Fetch and summarise the quiz results, and prepare the coding sheet.

The quizzes are defined in quiz-background.json and the two
quiz-knowledge-*.json files; all four files are tangled from the same
literate source (the appendix of the paper).  The results come straight
from Canvas via canvaslms; the item titles are the column keys.

Requires canvaslms (and its configured credentials); the --llm option
additionally requires the llm package with a configured model.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import csv
import json
import os
import statistics
import sys

<<the course and the item titles are the keys>>
<<fetch the results from Canvas>>
<<tabulate the closed items>>
<<pre-code the open answers with an LLM>>
<<write the coding sheet>>
<<analysis main>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

The course name and the item titles are the join keys between the
tangled files: if a title changes in a quiz, this dictionary is the
only other place to change---and they live in the same source, side by
side.
Canvas prefixes the report's columns with item identifiers, so we match
columns by containment rather than equality.

<<the course and the item titles are the keys>>=
COURSE = "prgi26"
QUIZ_TITLES = {
    "background": "Diagnostic quiz: your programming background",
    "start": "Concept quiz: what you know already",
    "end": "Concept quiz: what you know now",
}
STUDENT = "name"
CLOSED_ITEMS = ["Consent", "How you learned", "Confidence", "Preparation"]
OPEN_ITEMS = ["Previous programming", "Debugging exercise"]
CODED_KEYS = ["languages", "experience", "python",
              "contrast", "generalisation", "fusion"]
CODING_COLUMNS = CODED_KEYS + ["notes"]
SUGGESTED_COLUMNS = [f"suggested_{key}" for key in CODED_KEYS + ["rationale"]]


def column_for(title, fieldnames):
    """Return the report column whose name contains the item title."""
    return next((name for name in fieldnames if title in name), None)
@

\subsubsection{Fetching the report from Canvas}

Just as we create the quizzes with [[canvaslms]], we extract the
results with it: the same package provides the credentials, the course
lookup and the report machinery, so the program reuses them instead of
reimplementing the Canvas API calls.
The report is generated on demand by Canvas, so we request it, wait for
it, and download the CSV it points to.

<<fetch the results from Canvas>>=
def fetch_results(which):
    """Download the student-analysis rows for the start or end quiz."""
    import appdirs
    import requests
    import canvaslms.cli
    import canvaslms.cli.login
    from canvasapi import Canvas
    from canvaslms.cli.quizzes import (
        create_new_quiz_report,
        filter_quizzes,
        is_new_quiz,
        poll_progress,
    )

    config_file = os.path.join(
        appdirs.user_config_dir("canvaslms"), "config.json"
    )
    config = canvaslms.cli.read_configuration(config_file)
    hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)
    if not hostname.startswith("http"):
        hostname = f"https://{hostname}"
    canvas = Canvas(hostname, token)

    course = next(
        c for c in canvas.get_courses()
        if COURSE in (c.name or "") or COURSE in (c.course_code or "")
    )
    quiz = next(filter_quizzes([course], QUIZ_TITLES[which]))
    if not is_new_quiz(quiz):
        sys.exit(f"quiz {quiz.title!r} is not a New Quiz")

    progress = poll_progress(
        create_new_quiz_report(course, quiz.id, canvas._Canvas__requester)
    )
    url = progress.results.get("url") if progress else None
    if not url:
        sys.exit("Canvas did not produce the student-analysis report")
    response = requests.get(url)
    response.raise_for_status()
    return list(csv.DictReader(response.text.splitlines()))
@

\subsubsection{Summaries and the coding sheet}

Tabulating a closed item is counting its answer frequencies; we print
one table per item present in the report (the preparation item only
exists in the end quiz).
For the knowledge quizzes Canvas grades the items, so their reports
carry a score column, which we summarise.

<<tabulate the closed items>>=
def tabulate(rows, item):
    """Print the answer frequencies for one closed item."""
    column = column_for(item, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    counts = collections.Counter(row[column] for row in rows)
    print(f"\n{item}")
    for answer, count in counts.most_common():
        print(f"  {answer or '(no answer)'}: {count}")


def summarize_scores(rows):
    """Print the score distribution, if the report has scores."""
    column = column_for("score", rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    scores = [float(row[column]) for row in rows if row[column]]
    if scores:
        print(f"\nscores: n={len(scores)}"
              f", mean={statistics.mean(scores):.1f}"
              f", median={statistics.median(scores):.1f}"
              f", max={max(scores):.0f}")
@

The model suggests the codes from the two open answers: the languages,
the rough amount of experience and whether Python is among the
languages from the first, and which of the three patterns of variation
the described steps exhibit from the second.
The prompt defines the patterns briefly; the model must answer in JSON
so that the suggestions land in columns.
The suggestions are exactly that---\cref{analysis} requires a human
coder, and the verified codes go in the bare columns.

<<pre-code the open answers with an LLM>>=
CODING_PROMPT = """\
You are pre-coding survey answers for a study on debugging.
Answer in JSON with keys "languages" (comma-separated list of the
programming languages mentioned), "experience" (roughly how much
programming experience, in the student's own words, e.g. "2 years"),
"python" ("yes", "no" or "unclear"), "contrast", "generalisation",
"fusion" (each 0 or 1), and "rationale" (one sentence).

"languages", "experience" and "python" concern the languages answer.
The pattern codes concern the debugging-exercise answer, in which the
student describes how they would find out why a small program prints
the wrong thing:
"contrast" = compares the failing case against a working one;
"generalisation" = varies other things to see if the problem persists,
keeping the suspected cause fixed;
"fusion" = reconciles the found cause with prior understanding,
varying several things together.

Languages answer: {previous}
Debugging-exercise answer: {exercise}
"""


def llm_precode(rows):
    """Ask the default llm model to suggest codes for each row."""
    import llm

    model = llm.get_model()
    suggestions = []
    for row in rows:
        prompt = CODING_PROMPT.format(
            previous=row.get(column_for(OPEN_ITEMS[0], row.keys()) or "", ""),
            exercise=row.get(column_for(OPEN_ITEMS[1], row.keys()) or "", ""),
        )
        try:
            suggestion = json.loads(model.prompt(prompt).text())
        except (json.JSONDecodeError, Exception):
            suggestion = {}
        suggestions.append(
            {f"suggested_{key}": suggestion.get(key, "")
             for key in CODED_KEYS + ["rationale"]}
        )
    return suggestions
@

The coding sheet holds the open-text answers, the model's suggested
codes when [[--llm]] was given, and the empty columns for the human
coder's verified codes.

<<write the coding sheet>>=
def write_coding_sheet(rows, path, suggestions=None):
    """Write open-text answers with coding columns to path."""
    open_columns = {
        item: column_for(item, rows[0].keys()) for item in OPEN_ITEMS
    }
    fields = ([STUDENT] + OPEN_ITEMS
              + (SUGGESTED_COLUMNS if suggestions else []) + CODING_COLUMNS)
    with open(path, "w", newline="") as out:
        writer = csv.DictWriter(out, fieldnames=fields, extrasaction="ignore")
        writer.writeheader()
        for index, row in enumerate(rows):
            record = {STUDENT: row.get(STUDENT, "")}
            for item, column in open_columns.items():
                record[item] = row.get(column, "") if column else ""
            if suggestions:
                record.update(suggestions[index])
            writer.writerow(record)
@

The main function ties the pieces together: fetch (or read) the
report, tabulate and summarise, and---for the background quiz, whose
open items are the ones that get coded---pre-code if asked to and write
the sheet.

<<analysis main>>=
def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--quiz", choices=QUIZ_TITLES, default="background",
                        help="which quiz to analyse")
    parser.add_argument("--results",
                        help="read this CSV instead of fetching from Canvas")
    parser.add_argument("--llm", action="store_true",
                        help="pre-code the open answers with an LLM")
    parser.add_argument("--coding-sheet", default="quiz-coding-sheet.csv",
                        help="output CSV for the coding of open answers")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if args.results:
        with open(args.results, newline="") as infile:
            rows = list(csv.DictReader(infile))
    else:
        rows = fetch_results(args.quiz)
    if not rows:
        sys.exit("no responses")

    print(f"{len(rows)} students")
    for item in CLOSED_ITEMS:
        tabulate(rows, item)
    summarize_scores(rows)
    if args.quiz == "background":
        suggestions = llm_precode(rows) if args.llm else None
        write_coding_sheet(rows, args.coding_sheet, suggestions)
        print(f"\ncoding sheet written to {args.coding_sheet}")
@
